There is the abomination called 'lazy malloc', which is an
implementation-dependent method of implimenting malloc.

Under traditional malloc, once you attempt to malloc() memory, the
memory is either set aside for your program or the memory cannot be
allocated and a null pointer is returned.

Under lazy malloc, once you attempt to malloc() some memory, the 
system lies and reports that memory as allocated.  When you 
attempt to access that memory, then the operating system attempts 
to allocate it.  If it has no memory free to use, it either waits 
or kills the program.  (Cute, huh?)

The reasoning is that many poorly written programs allocate more 
memory then they ever use.  Lazy malloc limits memory usage.

The problem is when you have a system that uses memory as a 
adjustable-sized buffer:  Such a system could normally try to 
make a smaller buffer or free up older buffers if malloc() returned 
NULL.  Under lazy malloc, other things happen.

